# {{VIBE4DOCK_PROJECT_NAME}}

Minimal Vibe4Dock project template.

## Start

```bash
cp .env.local.example .env.local
docker compose up -d --build
```

## Endpoints

This project uses the **{{VIBE4DOCK_ROUTING}}** routing mode.

{{!PORTS_ONLY}}### Ports routing mode
{{!PORTS_ONLY}}
{{!PORTS_ONLY}}Each endpoint is published on its own host port:
{{!PORTS_ONLY}}
{{!PORTS_ONLY}}- App: `http://localhost:{{VIBE4DOCK_WEB_HOST_PORT}}`
{{!PORTS_ONLY}}- Tools: `http://localhost:{{VIBE4DOCK_TOOLS_HOST_PORT}}` (`TOOLS_USERNAME` + `TOOLS_PASSWORD`, if both set in `.env.local`)
{{!PORTS_ONLY}}- Root shell: `http://localhost:{{VIBE4DOCK_ROOT_SHELL_HOST_PORT}}` (`ROOT_SHELL_USERNAME` + `ROOT_SHELL_PASSWORD`, if both set in `.env.local`)
{{!PORTS_ONLY}}- App shell: `http://localhost:{{VIBE4DOCK_APP_SHELL_HOST_PORT}}` (`APP_SHELL_USERNAME` + `APP_SHELL_PASSWORD`, if both set in `.env.local`)
{{!PORTS_ONLY}}
{{!PATHS_ONLY}}### Paths routing mode
{{!PATHS_ONLY}}
{{!PATHS_ONLY}}A single HTTP port serves everything through a built-in nginx proxy. All endpoints live under reserved `{{VIBE4DOCK_BASE_PATH_PREFIX}}-*` paths:
{{!PATHS_ONLY}}
{{!PATHS_ONLY}}- App: `http://localhost:{{VIBE4DOCK_WEB_HOST_PORT}}/`
{{!PATHS_ONLY}}- Tools: `http://localhost:{{VIBE4DOCK_WEB_HOST_PORT}}{{VIBE4DOCK_BASE_PATH_DASHBOARD}}` (`TOOLS_USERNAME` + `TOOLS_PASSWORD`, if both set in `.env.local`)
{{!PATHS_ONLY}}- Root shell: `http://localhost:{{VIBE4DOCK_WEB_HOST_PORT}}{{VIBE4DOCK_BASE_PATH_ROOT_SHELL}}` (`ROOT_SHELL_USERNAME` + `ROOT_SHELL_PASSWORD`, if both set in `.env.local`)
{{!PATHS_ONLY}}- App shell: `http://localhost:{{VIBE4DOCK_WEB_HOST_PORT}}{{VIBE4DOCK_BASE_PATH_APP_SHELL}}` (`APP_SHELL_USERNAME` + `APP_SHELL_PASSWORD`, if both set in `.env.local`)
{{!PATHS_ONLY}}
{{!PATHS_ONLY}}Browser-capable addons (code-server, Adminer, Mailpit, Lazygit Shell, ...) are served automatically under `/{{VIBE4DOCK_BASE_PATH_PREFIX}}-<name>` paths. Addons without path support (for example OneDev) keep their own host ports. The `{{VIBE4DOCK_BASE_PATH_PREFIX}}-` namespace is reserved - do not use URLs starting with `/{{VIBE4DOCK_BASE_PATH_PREFIX}}-` in the web project itself.
{{!PATHS_ONLY}}

Use `docker/bash.sh` or `docker/root.sh` to enter the container.

The generated base project is intentionally neutral. It contains the Vibe4Dock engine and settings system, but no bundled tool or addon packs by default.

Add tool and addon packs by placing JSON definitions under `docker/tools/category/`. Optional template payloads can live under `docker/tools/package-templates/`.

Configure usernames and passwords only in `.env.local`. Commented or empty values stay inactive; uncommented, non-empty credentials are used automatically.

Addon services manage their own credentials through the Addons section of the tools UI (for example `CHAT_USERNAME` / `CHAT_PASSWORD` for the Chat addon). These values live in the service environment of `docker-compose.override.yml`, not in `.env.local`.

## Security features

The example includes optional login protection for:

- Tools UI (HTTP Basic Auth)
- Application shell (paths routing: form login with a signed session cookie served through the proxy; ports routing: HTTP Basic Auth provided by `ttyd`)
- Root shell (same behavior as the Application shell)
- Chat (paths routing: form login with a signed session cookie served through the proxy; the chat service itself stays credential-free)

All credentials are controlled only through `.env.local`. Addon-provided endpoints (such as the Chat UI) read their credentials from the addon settings (`addon_configs.json`, editable in the Tools UI) with a fallback to `.env.local`.

Protection is active only when both username and password are present and non-empty; commented lines are ignored. The Tools UI only reads and displays the protection status; it does not manage these credentials.

Example:

```dotenv
TOOLS_USERNAME="tools"
TOOLS_PASSWORD="change-me-tools"

APP_SHELL_USERNAME="application"
APP_SHELL_PASSWORD="change-me-application"

ROOT_SHELL_USERNAME="root"
ROOT_SHELL_PASSWORD="change-me-root"

CHAT_USERNAME="chat"
CHAT_PASSWORD="change-me-chat"
```

Rules:

- a login is active only when both username and password are present and non-empty
- commented lines are ignored
- the Tools UI only reads and displays the protection status; it does not manage these credentials

Restart requirements:

- after changing `TOOLS_*`, run `docker compose restart tools`
- after changing `APP_SHELL_*` or `ROOT_SHELL_*`, run `docker compose restart web`
- after changing `CHAT_*`, run `docker compose restart tools web` (the chat form login is served by the web container)

Notes:

- the Tools UI login title is `Vibe4Dock Login`
- in paths routing mode the shells and the chat show a form login page (realm-free, browser cache friendly); each service keeps its own session cookie for 30 days
- in ports routing mode the browser login dialog for the shells is provided by `ttyd` and uses its own default title

## Rebuild watcher (host daemon)

This project ships an opt-in rebuild watcher as a host daemon. The Tools UI's **Manual Rebuild Required** banner exposes a **Rebuild now** button that asks the watcher to run `./docker/rebuild.sh` for you. By default the watcher also runs the rebuild automatically whenever a tool install marks the hint file.

```bash
./docker/watcher.sh start    # host daemon (Linux / macOS)
./docker/watcher.sh status
./docker/watcher.sh logs
./docker/watcher.sh stop
```

On Windows:

```bat
.\docker\watcher.bat run
.\docker\watcher.bat status
```

To run it as a real OS service (recommended):

```bash
sudo ./docker/watcher.sh service | sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/vibe4dock-watcher.service
sudo systemctl enable --now vibe4dock-watcher.service
```

On macOS save the matching `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.vibe4dock.watcher.plist` (see the main README for the template) and `launchctl load -w` it.

Set `WATCHER_AUTO_REBUILD=0` in `.env.local` to keep the watcher in pure-logging mode. The button in the UI still works, but the watcher will only log the request - you have to run `./docker/rebuild.sh` manually.
